r the cite655†CSS declaration block .\nL1505:   8. Return `value`.\nL1506: The `parentRule` attribute must return the cite604†parent CSS rule .\nL1507: \nL1508: The `cssFloat` attribute, on getting, must return the result of invoking `cite703†getPropertyValue() ` with `float` as argument. On setting, the attribute must invoke `cite705†setProperty() ` with `float` as first argument, as second argument the given value, and no third argument. Any exceptions thrown must be re-thrown.\nL1509: For each CSS property `property` that is a cite125†supported CSS property , the following partial interface applies where `camel-cased attribute` is obtained by running the cite807†CSS property to IDL attribute algorithm for `property`.\nL1510: \nL1511: Tests\nL1512:   * cite808†change-rule-with-layers-crash.html†wpt.fyi cite809†(live test)†wpt.live cite810†(source)†github.com L1513:   * cite811†style-attr-update-across-documents.html†wpt.fyi cite812†(live test)†wpt.live cite813†(source)†github.com L1514: \nL1515:     partial interface cite578†CSSStyleProperties {\nL1516:       [cite699†CEReactions†html.spec.whatwg.org ] attribute [cite168†LegacyNullToEmptyString†webidl.spec.whatwg.org ] cite169†CSSOMString `_camel_cased_attribute`;\nL1517:     };\nL1518: The cite814†`camel-cased attribute` attribute, on getting, must return the result of invoking `cite703†getPropertyValue() ` with the argument being the result of running the cite815†IDL attribute to CSS property algorithm for `camel-cased attribute`.\nL1519: Setting the cite814†`camel-cased attribute` attribute must invoke `cite705†setProperty() ` with the first argument being the result of running the cite815†IDL attribute to CSS property algorithm for `camel-cased attribute`, as second argument the given value, and no third argument. Any exceptions thrown must be re-thrown.\nL1520: \nL1521: For example, for the cite816†font-size property there would be a `fontSize` IDL attribute.\nL1522: For each CSS property `property` that is a cite125†supported CSS property and that begins with the string `-webkit-`, the following partial interface applies where `webkit-cased attribute` is obtained by running the cite807†CSS property to IDL attribute algorithm for `property`, with the lowercase first flag set.\nL1523: \nL1524:     partial interface cite578†CSSStyleProperties {\nL1525:       [cite699†CEReactions†html.spec.whatwg.org ] attribute [cite168†LegacyNullToEmptyString†webidl.spec.whatwg.org ] cite169†CSSOMString `_webkit_cased_attribute`;\nL1526:     };\nL1527: The ``webkit-cased attribute`` attribute, on getting, must return the result of invoking `cite703†getPropertyValue() ` with the argument being the result of running the cite815†IDL attribute to CSS property algorithm for `webkit-cased attribute`, with the dash prefix flag set.\nL1528: Setting the cite817†`webkit-cased attribute`†www.w3.org attribute must invoke `cite705†setProperty() ` with the first argument being the result of running the cite815†IDL attribute to CSS property algorithm for `webkit-cased attribute`, with the dash prefix flag set, as second argument the given value, and no third argument. Any exceptions thrown must be re-thrown.\nL1529: For example, if the user agent supports the cite818†-webkit-transform†compat.spec.whatwg.org property, there would be a `webkitTransform` IDL attribute. There would also be a `WebkitTransform` IDL attribute because of the rules for camel-cased attributes.\nL1530: For each CSS property `property` that is a cite125†supported CSS property , except for properties that have no \"`-`\" (U+002D) in the property name, the following partial interface applies where `dashed attribute` is `property`.\nL1531: \nL1532:     partial interface cite578†CSSStyleProperties {\nL1533:       [cite699†CEReactions†html.spec.whatwg.org ] attribute [cite168†LegacyNullToEmptyString†webidl.spec.whatwg.org ] cite169†CSSOMString `_dashed_attribute`;\nL1534:     };\nL1535: The ``dashed attribute`` attribute, on getting, must return the result of invoking `cite703†getPropertyValue() ` with the argument being `dashed attribute`.\nL1536: \nL1537: Setting the cite819†`dashed attribute` attribute must invoke `cite705†setProperty() ` with the first argument being `dashed attribute`, as second argument the given value, and no third argument. Any exceptions thrown must be re-thrown.\nL1538: For example, for the cite816†font-size property there would be a `font-size` IDL attribute. In JavaScript, the property can be accessed as follows, assuming `element` is an cite820†HTML element†html.spec.whatwg.org :\nL1539: \nL1540:     `element`.style['font-size'];\nL1541: \nL1542: The CSS property to IDL attribute algorithm for `property`, optionally with a lowercase first flag set, is as follows:\nL1543:   1. Let `output` be the empty string.\nL1544:   2. Let `uppercase next` be unset.\nL1545:   3. If the lowercase first flag is set, remove the first character from `property`.\nL1546:   4. For each character `c` in `property`:\nL1547:     1. If `c` is \"`-`\" (U+002D), let `uppercase next` be set.\nL1548:     2. Otherwise, if `uppercase next` is set, let `uppercase next` be unset and append `c` cite821†converted to ASCII uppercase†infra.spec.whatwg.org to `output`.\nL1549:     3. Otherwise, append `c` to `output`.\nL1550:   5. Return `output`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Linked Parameters Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-link-params-1/)\nciteturn1224view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1220view1\",\"lineno\":118}); Total lines: 446\nL70: CSS link parameters are a way to set CSS cite14†custom environment variables on an \"external\" resource, either by a CSS property or thru a special fragment scheme on the URL. This gives a limited, but powerful, subset of the customizability that \"inline\" SVG images have to \"external\" SVG images, or to other linked resources that use CSS to style themselves.\nL71: \nL72: A cite54†link parameter is a pair of a cite55†<dashed-ident>†www.w3.org name, and an arbitrary (possibly empty) cite56†<declaration-value>†www.w3.org value.\nL73: For example, an SVG image can be written to use cite54†link parameters , allowing it to have its colors changed on the fly, like:\nL74: \nL75:     <svg>\nL76:       <path fill=\"env(--color, black)\" d=\"...\" />\nL77:     </svg>\nL78: By default, it will fill its shape with black, as that’s the fallback color specified. But cite54†link parameters can customize the color in several ways:\nL79: \nL80:     <img src=\"image.svg#:~:param(--color,green)\">\nL81: \nL82: \nL83:     img {\nL84:       link-parameters: param(--color, green);\nL85:     }\nL86: \nL87: \nL88:     .foo {\nL89:       background-image: url(\"image.svg\", param(--color, green));\nL90:     }\nL91: ## 2. Setting a Link Parameter\nL92: \nL93: An external resource can be accompanied by a list of cite54†link parameters , a cite57†struct†infra.spec.whatwg.org composed of:\nL94: \nL95:   * a name, usually a cite55†<dashed-ident>†www.w3.org L96: \nL97:   * an optional type, a cite58†<css-type>†www.w3.org L98: \nL99:   * a value (potentially empty), a CSS value\nL100: \nL101: There are three ways to specify a cite54†link parameter :\nL102:   * via the cite59†link-parameters property, which applies to the resource itself (if the element represents an external resource), and to all external resources used in CSS properties on the element\nL103: \nL104:   * via a cite60†fragment directive†wicg.github.io directly in the URL of an external resource.\nL105: \nL106:   * via a cite61†param() argument in a cite62†<url>†www.w3.org L107: \nL108: If specified in multiple of these ways, all of the cite54†link parameters are appended into a single list for the external resource, in the order:\nL109:   1. the cite59†link-parameters property on the element, if relevant\nL110: \nL111:   2. the cite63†link parameter directives of the URL\nL112: \nL113:   3. the cite61†param() cite64†<url-modifier>†www.w3.org s in cite65†url()†www.w3.org L114: \nL115: If multiple cite54†link parameters exist with the same name, the last one in the list is used.\nL116: \nL117: How to access cite54†link parameters in the linked resource is defined in the next section, cite33†§ 3 Using Link Parameters .\nL118: ### 2.1. The cite61†param() Function\nL119: \nL120: All three methods of setting cite54†link parameters share the same syntax for defining a parameter, using the param() function:\nL121: \nL122:     param() = param( cite66†<param-spec> cite67†,†www.w3.org cite56†<declaration-value>†www.w3.org cite68†?†www.w3.org )\nL123:     cite66†<param-spec> = color cite69†|†www.w3.org accent-color cite69†|†www.w3.org [ cite55†<dashed-ident>†www.w3.org cite58†<css-type>†www.w3.org cite68†?†www.w3.org ]\nL124: Depending on the cite66†<param-spec> , the cite61†param() function defines a cite54†link parameter in slightly different ways:\nL125: \nL126: color\nL127: \nL128: \nL129:   * cite70†Name : `color`\nL130: \nL131:   * cite71†Type : cite72†<color>†www.w3.org L132: \nL133:   * cite73†Value : The second argument, if specified. Otherwise, the value of cite74†color†www.w3.org on the element.\nL134: \nL135: accent-color\nL136: \nL137: \nL138:   * cite70†Name : `accent-color`\nL139: \nL140:   * cite71†Type : cite72†<color>†www.w3.org L141:   * cite73†Value : The second argument, if specified. Otherwise, the value of cite75†accent-color†www.w3.org on the element.\nL142: \nL143: cite55†<dashed-ident>†www.w3.org L144: \nL145: \nL146:   * cite70†Name : the cite55†<dashed-ident>†www.w3.org L147: \nL148:   * cite71†Type : the cite58†<css-type>†www.w3.org , if specified.\nL149: \nL150:   * cite73†Value : The second argument, if specified. Otherwise, the value of the cite76†custom property†www.w3.org with the specified name on the element.\nL151: Note that a completely empty second argument is valid, and is indicated by specifying the comma separating it from the first argument.\nL152: \nL153: That is, param(--foo) indicates that the cite54†link parameter is using the default value, drawing from the --foo cite76†custom property†www.w3.org on the element.\nL154: \nL155: On the other hand, param(--foo,) explicitly gives the cite54†link parameter an empty value.\nL156: \nL157: This is similar to var(--foo) having no fallback, but var(--foo,) having an empty fallback.\nL158: If the cite54†link parameter has a cite71†type , the cite73†value (default or manually provided) is parsed as that type. If it fails to parse as the given type, the link parameter is invalid and ignored. (The cite61†param() defining it is not invalid.)\nL159: Note: Authors can specify fallbacks by repeating a single cite61†param() multiple times, like param(--foo <color>, blue), param(--foo <color>, xztheta(1 2 3)), which will use the (currently undefined) xztheta() color function if it’s supported and cite77†blue†www.w3.org in user agents that don’t support that function, similar to specifying a property multiple times.\nL160: A cite61†param() function usually has an element context from the way it’s used. If present, the cite73†value uses that context (and the cite71†type , if present) to fully resolve itself to a cite78†used value†www.w3.org before being passed into the linked resource. If the value needs an element context to fully resolve, but doesn’t have one, the cite54†link parameter is invalid and ignored.\nL161: For example, param(--foo <color>, currentcolor) will use the element context to resolve to a specific color, rather than somewhat uselessly passing as the keyword.\nL162: \nL163: Even without a cite71†type , cite79†arbitrary substitution functions†www.w3.org will attempt to resolve in the cite73†value , and might need an element context (for example, cite80†var()†www.w3.org and cite81†attr()†www.w3.org both do).\nL164: Other values will be left unresolved without a cite71†type ; for example, param(--foo, currentcolor) will pass the cite82†currentcolor†www.w3.org keyword itself, without resolving it as a cite72†<color>†www.w3.org , and thus doesn’t need an element context.\nL165: \nL166: Similarly, if a cite61†param() doesn’t provide a second argument, and doesn’t have an element context, the cite54†link parameter is invalid and ignored. (Because the default draws on the value of a property on the element.)\nL167: ### 2.2. For An Entire Element: the cite59†link-parameters property\nL168: \nL169: Name:  | link-parameters\nL170: cite83†Value:†www.w3.org | none cite69†|†www.w3.org cite61†<param()> cite84†#†www.w3.org L171: cite85†Initial:†www.w3.org | none\nL172: cite86†Applies to:†www.w3.org | all elements and pseudo-elements\nL173: cite87†Inherited:†www.w3.org | no\nL174: cite88†Percentages:†www.w3.org | n/a\nL175: cite89†Computed value:†www.w3.org | as specified\nL176: cite90†Canonical order:†www.w3.org | per grammar\nL177: cite91†Animation type:†www.w3.org | discrete\nL178: The cite59†link-parameters property is one way to set cite54†link parameters on the element itself (if it is an element representing an external resource, such as an HTML `cite52†img†html.spec.whatwg.org ` or `cite53†iframe†html.spec.whatwg.org `), and on all external CSS resources specified on the element (such as background images, etc). Its values are:\nL179: \nL180: none\nL181: \nL182: \nL183: No cite54†link parameters are specified.\nL184: \nL185: cite61†<param()> #\nL186: \nL187: \nL188: A list of one or more cite54†link parameters .\nL189: The cite61†param() functions used in cite59†link-parameters use the element they’re set on as the element context.\nL190: \nL191: Note: If there are multiple images on the given element (content image and/or CSS images like backgrounds), they’ll all recieve all the values in cite59†link-parameters , even if they don’t use them. This is generally safe, as you need to use cite92†env()†www.w3.org to access a cite54†link parameter in the linked resource, tho cite93†color and cite94†accent-color do have additional side-effects.\nL192: ### 2.3. For A Single URL: the cite64†<url-modifier>†www.w3.org L193: \nL194: While cite59†link-parameters sets cite54†link parameters for every URL on an element, sometimes you want to set parameters for only one resource, or use the same resource multiple times on an element with different parameters. For this, you can use one or more cite61†param() functions directly in the cite62†<url>†www.w3.org specifying the linked resource, as a cite64†<url-modifier>†www.w3.org .\nL195: For example, if the site is using a --primary-color custom property to theme its elements with, and wanted an SVG background using env(--color) to reflect it, it could write:\nL196: \nL197:     .foo {\nL198:       background-image: url(\nL199:         \"http://example.com/image.svg\"\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1812163 - (css-link-params-1) [META] Implement CSS Linked Parameters (context properties standard replacement) (https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1812163)\nciteturn1224view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1812163\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 591\n"
